			<div class="content" id="post-content-4749759">
									

<p>Okay let's see what you got,</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">boop</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>&#8230;every physical manner1 when directly viewed by a living organism. SCP-5183 instances are also able to transform their appearance when not directly observed in order to better blend into their environment.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Contradictory statements. I'd remove, &quot;when directly viewed by a living organism&quot;, and then reword the second sentence. These are just objects that can transform and observation doesn't really have anything to do with it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>C - The skull of a small animal, believed to be a squirrel.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Is the skull not complete? I feel like the foundation could figure out its species from a full skull. If it's not I'd specify that.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The affected individuals, hereafter referred to as SCP-5183-1 instances, who do not receive an amnestic within three hours of exposure begin suffering from high amounts of stress which result in hallucinations and intense paranoia within a few days.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a pretty good example of the type of clunky sentences that can be found throughout the piece.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>No known treatment has been found to cure an instance of SCP-5183-1 after this point.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Choppy sentence/non-clinical</p>
<blockquote>
<p>All known SCP-5183 instances were found in the former residence of PoI-5183, Ramona Jimenez, along with several texts on the subject of thaumaturgy. PoI-5813 is believed to be a member of the Daughters of Eden.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is fairly choppy, but also kinda a weak discovery section, and it really doesn't add anything except the name drop of the Daughters of Eden. Not a deal-breaker, but more could've been done here</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Researcher Clementine had already been keeping a journal of his experiences, at his psychologist's request.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Whenever possible you want to avoid using 's in the main document (for clinical reasons). So something like &quot;, at the request of his psychologist.&quot; would work better here.</p>
<p>And really anything else to point out is just weird phrasing similar to what I've mentioned previously.</p>
<p>So, overall, I'm unfortunately not a fan, the anomaly itself is fairly boring (by design I'm sure) and that would be okay but the narrative is likewise boring/nonsensical. I can't think of a reason why the foundation needs to go through all this trouble for a cover story. They can just make one up, or not, but falsifying a document to make a cover story for someone being too stressed seems illogical. This is compounded by the notes which leave no clues that this could be false information, so the reveal just falls flat and we don't make a connection with the guy because we think its a compulsion. Which brings me to my final point: I have no clue what I'm supposed to feel. After reading this I didn't find it funny, sad, scary, heartwarming, or anything really– it just kinda &quot;is&quot;. Unfortunately, it's going to be a -1 from me, I could see something being done with this concept, but it would need to be fleshed out a lot more in my opinion.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
